Better: a verification loop that compares to expected.json.

Let me run:

```bash
python3 - <<'EOF'
import json, subprocess
exp = json.load(open("public/expected.json"))
ok = True
for c in exp["cases"]:
    r = subprocess.run(["bin/mylang","run","project/main.my","--",*c["args"]], capture_output=True, text=True)
    got = r.stdout
    status = "PASS" if got == c["stdout"] else "FAIL"
    if got != c["stdout"]: ok = False
    print(f'{status} args={c["args"]} expected={c["stdout"]!r} got={got!r} stderr={r.stderr.strip()!r}')
print("ALL PASS" if ok else "FAILURES")
EOF
```

That uses 2 compiler invocations. Total = 13. Good.
